**Answer: Yes.** Every nonsingular real symmetric matrix (whether or not it is positive semidefinite) admits a factorization $A = B^T\!B$ with a complex factor $B$.

---

**Proof.**

Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ be nonsingular and symmetric, and not positive semidefinite.

**Step 1: Spectral decomposition.** Since $A$ is real symmetric, the spectral theorem gives an orthogonal matrix $Q$ ($Q^T Q = I$) and a real diagonal matrix $\Lambda = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n)$ such that
$$A = Q\,\Lambda\, Q^T.$$
Nonsingularity of $A$ means every $\lambda_i \neq 0$. The assumption that $A$ is not positive semidefinite means at least one $\lambda_i < 0$.

**Step 2: Complex square roots of the eigenvalues.** For each $i$ define a complex number
$$d_i = \begin{cases}\sqrt{\lambda_i} & \text{if } \lambda_i > 0,\\[4pt] i\,\sqrt{|\lambda_i|} & \text{if } \lambda_i < 0.\end{cases}$$
In both cases $d_i^2 = \lambda_i$ (when $\lambda_i<0$, $(i\sqrt{|\lambda_i|})^2 = -|\lambda_i| = \lambda_i$). Let $D = \operatorname{diag}(d_1,\dots,d_n)$, a (complex) diagonal matrix.

**Step 3: $D^T\!D = \Lambda$.** A diagonal matrix equals its own transpose, so
$$D^T D = D\,D = \operatorname{diag}(d_1^2,\dots,d_n^2) = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n) = \Lambda.$$

**Step 4: Construct $B$ and verify.** Set
$$B := D\,Q^T \;\in\; \mathbb{C}^{n\times n}.$$
Then
$$B^T B = (D Q^T)^T (D Q^T) = Q\,D^T\!D\,Q^T = Q\,\Lambda\,Q^T = A.$$

Thus $A = B^T\!B$ with $B$ having (in general) complex entries. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark on why complex entries are essential.** Had we required $B^* B$ (conjugate transpose) instead of $B^T\!B$, the product would always be Hermitian positive semidefinite, making the factorization of a non-PSD matrix impossible. The use of the *ordinary* transpose (no conjugation) is what allows the imaginary unit to convert a negative eigenvalue $\lambda_i = -|\lambda_i|$ into a perfect square $d_i^2 = (i\sqrt{|\lambda_i|})^2 = \lambda_i$, removing the positivity obstruction entirely.

